hello everybody producer pat ford here  filling in for david pakman on another  exciting edition of the david pakman  show fear not david is going to be back  on monday but for today at least on this  christmas eve edition of the show you  are all stuck with me but that's okay  because we're going to talk about a lot  of interesting stuff we're going to talk  about uh build back better we're going  to talk about joel manchin we're going  to talk about omicron and the latest  coveted surge so let's get right into  the news as we know this week joe  manchin effectively killed the build  back better legislation in its current  forum the 1.9 trillion dollar social  spending plan is not going to get passed  this year and it's not going to get  passed in its current forum uh mansion  went on fox news sunday with brett bear  and he told him look i'm just not going  to support this thing so as of now it's  dead in the water chief among mansion's  reasons for opposing the bill were that  he believed it would add to the national  debt he believed it would further  accelerate the rate of inflation in the  united states and he just gave this  general sentiment of look we're we're  spending so much money as it is right  now we can't possibly afford to spend  any more so some of this economic  fear-mongering stuff that we will often  hear from the centrists and the right  wing of this country of course depending  on the circumstances well goldman sachs  ran the numbers and they found that  actually not passing buildback better is  going to cost the us economy money  because the spending plan would infuse  60 billion dollars into the gross  domestic product a team of economists at  goldman predicted that the us gdp would  grow three percent in the first quarter  of 2022 if buildback better were passed  3.5  in the second quarter and another three  percent in the third quarter of 2022  that's again if buildback better was  signed into law this year now the  estimate uh without the spending plan in  place because they redid the numbers uh  is that two percent uh is going to be  the rate of growth for the first quarter  three percent is going to be the rate of  growth for the second quarter and 2.75  percent in the third quarter of 2022 and  when you line up all of those numbers  you compare the percentages you run the  raw numbers the differences add up to 60  billion dollars in economic losses for  the first three quarters of 2022 and of  course it's probably going to add up to  a lot more than that because it's only  counting for the first three quarters of  the year and obviously the impacts of  buildback better are going to extend far  beyond 2022. now of course we have to  include some caveats here like for  example just because we're not going to  get the 1.9 trillion dollar legislation  there's a good chance that we get  something done right i mean maybe it'll  be 500 billion maybe it'd be a trillion  maybe it'll be 1.5 trillion dollars we  just don't yet know what the total is  going to be and we don't know what  provisions are going to get passed yet  and unfortunately it seems to be really  up to mansion and what he wants to do  but we'll probably see some boost to the  gdp numbers compared to those more  conservative estimates second of all you  can never fully predict what the us  economy is going to do sometimes we hear  estimates from financial institutions or  government agencies about what we expect  job numbers to be or what we expect the  stock market to be at months down the  line years down the line and it's always  just a guess i mean you can make an  educated guess certainly but you never  know until it actually happens you can't  fully predict the us economy and what  it's going to do but these estimates  certainly do seem to jive with the  reality of the economy and what we know  about demand side stimulus because if  you think about it if you go to these  different provisions on the bill  one of the key provisions is extending  the child tax credit of three thousand  dollars or three thousand six hundred  dollars depending on the age of the  child uh per year um per child it works  out so that if people have this extra  money in their pocket they're going to  be able to spend it on things like going  out to dinner or going on vacation or  birthday presents or whatever the case  may be and the same can be said about  the other provisions too right because  people can save some money on the cost  of elder care or child care or  healthcare costs or prescription drug  costs and so with that extra money  people can go out and participate in the  economy and it can be economically  stimulative so i understand why this  conservative argument will sometimes win  the day because it just seems so  intuitive right like if we just don't  spend the money that means that we're  going to save the money but it's  actually quite the opposite and that's  exactly what these goldman sachs numbers  show us  and we of course know that republicans  are going to make this argument we know  that they love to fearmonger about  inflation and spending and the debt and  the deficit of course they only do that  when a democrat is in charge they don't  care so much about these things when a  republican is in the white house we saw  that last year with donald trump  compared to joe biden this year i mean  these these things like inflation and  spending they were all going on under  the trump administration last year only  now do they care about these things but  what's upsetting is that someone who is  supposed to be a democrat joe manchin he  also buys into these talking points and  ultimately that's going to harm the  people of west virginia and it's going  to harm the country as a whole because  it's going to cost the u.s economy  billions of dollars going into next year
 

